1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,066
Wrongful convictions in shared failure banks.

2
00:00:03,817 --> 00:00:07,847
Why the memory of failure in automated science should keep its evidence.

3
00:00:10,233 --> 00:00:13,925
Autonomous research agents increasingly share a failure bank.

4
00:00:14,775 --> 00:00:17,260
Every failed experiment is written down once,

5
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:19,845
so no agent wastes budget repeating it.

6
00:00:20,895 --> 00:00:25,140
That sounds sensible. But each failure verdict is a statistical claim,

7
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:28,319
usually made from one small, noisy experiment.

8
00:00:30,417 --> 00:00:33,787
Take an idea that is genuinely good, but only slightly.

9
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:38,320
Measure it once, and the result scatters around its small true effect.

10
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:43,540
About a third of the time, the measurement lands below zero.

11
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:46,535
The idea fails, and the bank marks it dead.

12
00:00:47,586 --> 00:00:50,609
Marginally good ideas are exactly the ones a

13
00:00:50,609 --> 00:00:53,632
single noisy test is most likely to convict.

14
00:00:55,717 --> 00:00:58,274
And the two possible errors are not symmetric.

15
00:00:58,924 --> 00:01:01,959
A bad idea that is wrongly kept gets built on.

16
00:01:01,959 --> 00:01:05,835
Its descendants keep failing, and the mistake exposes itself.

17
00:01:06,785 --> 00:01:10,380
A good idea that is wrongly killed is never tried again.

18
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:13,125
When new ideas grow from confirmed ones,

19
00:01:13,125 --> 00:01:15,660
none of its descendants are ever generated.

20
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:20,639
False positives expose themselves. False negatives never do.

21
00:01:22,733 --> 00:01:24,736
How often does this happen in practice?

22
00:01:25,386 --> 00:01:29,231
In a live training loop for a small character-level GPT,

23
00:01:29,231 --> 00:01:35,343
17.5% of convictions were wrongful, close to the 19% our theory predicts.

24
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:41,079
Among near-miss ideas, ones that passed once and then failed verification,

25
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:43,722
between a third and a half of the verdicts were wrong.

26
00:01:44,572 --> 00:01:47,727
And replaying 122,817 convictions on the

27
00:01:47,727 --> 00:01:51,472
public FCNet benchmark shows the same shape:

28
00:01:51,472 --> 00:01:55,191
the errors concentrate on small, genuine improvements.

29
00:01:57,283 --> 00:01:59,960
Do language-model agents look past the label?

30
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:02,955
We showed models the same one-run record,

31
00:02:02,955 --> 00:02:05,573
labeled either dead, or inconclusive.

32
00:02:06,323 --> 00:02:07,758
Labeled inconclusive,

33
00:02:07,758 --> 00:02:14,543
three Claude models put the chance the idea is genuinely better at 32.5%.

34
00:02:14,543 --> 00:02:15,603
Labeled dead: 8.5%.

35
00:02:16,553 --> 00:02:21,488
Across 12 models from 9 families, agents choosing their next experiment

36
00:02:21,488 --> 00:02:25,003
revisited none of 94 dead-marked ideas.

37
00:02:25,003 --> 00:02:28,318
And every family picked the best available

38
00:02:28,318 --> 00:02:30,462
idea less often once it carried the label.

39
00:02:31,312 --> 00:02:34,855
The models can price the evidence. The label overrides it.

40
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:40,180
The fix is a change of schema. It needs no new model.

41
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:44,785
Instead of a bare dead flag, each record carries its evidence:

42
00:02:44,785 --> 00:02:47,916
the number of runs, the measured effect, and the noise.

43
00:02:48,666 --> 00:02:53,951
From that, the bank computes the probability that the idea is truly dead.

44
00:02:53,951 --> 00:02:58,706
One run, slightly worse: 0.79. That is below the threshold,

45
00:02:58,706 --> 00:03:02,012
so the idea stays open, and a retry is allowed.

46
00:03:02,962 --> 00:03:08,727
Five runs, averaging the same deficit: 0.96. Now the lock is earned.

47
00:03:09,676 --> 00:03:11,551
It is a standard confidence rule.

48
00:03:11,551 --> 00:03:14,025
Shared failure banks just don't implement it.

49
00:03:16,117 --> 00:03:17,632
Does it matter end to end?

50
00:03:17,632 --> 00:03:21,112
We ran a closed research loop with real training runs:

51
00:03:21,112 --> 00:03:23,807
8 independent loops for each memory rule.

52
00:03:24,557 --> 00:03:28,322
With no memory, with binary locks, or with a parole rule,

53
00:03:28,322 --> 00:03:31,999
each loop found about 46 truth-validated discoveries.

54
00:03:32,848 --> 00:03:36,213
With evidence-graded records: 60.5.

55
00:03:36,213 --> 00:03:41,133
That is 30–32% more; a difference of 13.9,

56
00:03:41,133 --> 00:03:46,074
with a 95% interval from 9.7 to 18.1.

57
00:03:47,025 --> 00:03:48,750
It does not hold everywhere.

58
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:53,350
In a low-noise, short-horizon loop, binary locking edges ahead,

59
00:03:53,350 --> 00:03:57,456
and with language-model proposers, the ordering depends on the proposer.

60
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:03,046
Records that carry their evidence. Locks that require it.

61
00:04:03,996 --> 00:04:06,882
The code, logs, and cached runs are on GitHub.

